        "id": 1000018
      },
      "writtenOn": "2012-09-06T04:31:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If it was scl that was low, this doesn\u0027t do anything; at best this is waiting for a device, that is clock-stretching, to release scl.  Of course, since we also drive the clock low here, we won\u0027t see this until after we release SCL.\n\nInstead, I suggest if scl is low here, that we log the condition, and poll scl pin while waiting some max \u0027device clock stretching time\u0027.  And, if it is still low, then all we can do is log an error message, and limp along w/out i2c.",
      "revId": "e54ac8da053cd525946d9c7410405fe1686c5400",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAAPn//z8w\u003d",
        "filename": "chip/stm32/i2c.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 453,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000107
      },
      "writtenOn": "2012-09-06T15:34:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Agreed that it won\u0027t do anything if it was SCL that was low.  ...but at least it won\u0027t be harmful.\n\nNote that we\u0027re only running in cold boot.  The hope is that the time between the reset happening and this code running is long enough to account for any \"reasonable\" clock stretch.  Hard to know how much longer to wait...  ...but could do another 10ms wait here if we wanted.\n\nWe can\u0027t really limp along without i2c.  ...the PMIC code will later fail and hard reboot everything.",
      "parentUuid": "AAAAPn//0OU\u003d",
      "revId": "e54ac8da053cd525946d9c7410405fe1686c5400",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
